[THOUGHT] Vital signs confirm hyperthermia (39.6°C), tachycardia (116/min), and normotension (126/66 mmHg) — consistent with early or evolving NMS. The absence of reported hypertension or labile BP does not exclude NMS, as autonomic features can be variable. However, critical neurological exam elements — especially muscle tone (to confirm generalized rigidity), reflexes (to assess for hyperreflexia or clonus), and mental status (to quantify altered mentation) — are missing and are essential to support the diagnosis and rule out mimics (e.g., encephalitis, metabolic encephalopathy). Since these were not provided in the initial data pull, I must now explicitly request them as a targeted test — they are high-yield and directly address domain #7.

[TEST] Perform and report:  
- Muscle tone assessment in bilateral upper and lower limbs (describe as normal, increased [lead-pipe or cogwheel], or decreased);  
- Deep tendon reflexes: patellar and biceps (graded 0–4+);  
- Presence/absence of ankle clonus;  
- Mental status using Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score (E + V + M components separately).

